#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338932 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "65c88cb4-d780-590b-b992-1f5a2d099b51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x+y=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336940} {:answer/id #uuid "b17a59bd-34cb-524b-a586-d668f0ab265c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x-y=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337621} {:answer/id #uuid "b93bd652-ee1e-5354-8880-3331e0abdb27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337674} {:answer/id #uuid "c3e5d67c-091f-5e66-b35b-d7bf5ddf3a1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337771} {:answer/id #uuid "fcd19af6-18df-5dbc-8ca0-9ef0b733476e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338255}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338255} :answer-field/id #uuid "947eebdf-2bd7-5433-b16d-dbcb38a79114" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338552}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "05c970aa-8c53-5d2c-be67-cb9e3da21e11" :question/math-academy-id "q-71385" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4.$  Find the equation of the normal line to the curve at the point $(2,0).$" :question/worked-solution "If we set $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=4.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(2,0)$ can be found by computing $∇f(2,0).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(2,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =2x\\,i+2y\\,j \\\\ ∇f(2,0) & =2(2)\\,i+2(0)\\,j \\\\  & =4\\,i \\\\  & =⟨4,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇f(2,0)=⟨4,0⟩$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(2,0).$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-{x}_{0}){v}_{y}-(y-{y}_{0}){v}_{x} & =0 \\\\ (x-2)0-(y-0)4 & =0 \\\\ -4y & =0 \\\\ y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338933 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0dfa2d9c-c5a6-5bae-b0d2-a5aba5899793" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x+y=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336165} {:answer/id #uuid "521e5638-e80d-5edb-bd60-91e3b7a98567" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x-2y=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336787} {:answer/id #uuid "9255b74e-b684-545c-a101-b50d053e917e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x-y=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337362} {:answer/id #uuid "928ea3e2-97a0-57df-ae69-6fb23ba7d7dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x+2y=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337365} {:answer/id #uuid "c360c2bb-962a-5e23-b0d5-b54534c74210" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y-x=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337766}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186337362} :answer-field/id #uuid "59dddcfe-8a5c-5160-b7d7-f42966cf2e3f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338431}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "2de4f7e4-2884-5220-a39a-17359a9ea964" :question/math-academy-id "q-71388" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $2x+y-1=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}.$  Find the equation of the normal line to the curve at the point $(1,0).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+y-1 & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}} \\\\ -1 & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}-2x-y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}-2x-y$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=-1.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(1,0)$ can be found by computing $∇f(1,0).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(1,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}-2x-y)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}-2x-y)\\,j \\\\  & =(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}-2)\\,i+(-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}-1)\\,j \\\\ ∇f(1,0) & =(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}-{0}^{2}}}-2)\\,i+(-\\frac{0}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}-{0}^{2}}}-1)\\,j \\\\  & =-i-j \\\\  & =⟨-1,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇f(1,0)=⟨-1,-1⟩$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(1,0).$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}} & =\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}} \\\\ \\frac{x-1}{-1} & =\\frac{y-0}{-1} \\\\ 1-x & =-y \\\\ x-y & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338934 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "241c7529-7f80-5365-a0c6-f7e836aaee88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3y+x+10=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336372} {:answer/id #uuid "391f7649-2e76-52fa-81b6-233716b6923c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y+3x+4=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336569} {:answer/id #uuid "6cb41a0a-f9a7-5281-9885-444e698af19b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3y-x+8=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336998} {:answer/id #uuid "b3143694-8d1c-519f-bf94-4aa69b2c3b0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3y+2x+11=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337629} {:answer/id #uuid "f204000c-6f32-5823-8c04-611b3011d48d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y+3x+20=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338150}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186336372} :answer-field/id #uuid "4287e815-efb6-5dc2-9403-e74ef29f8446" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338397}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f1df9544-1831-5471-b207-bb07d395022e" :question/math-academy-id "q-71390" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve ${y}^{2}+3{x}^{2}=4xy.$  Find the equation of the normal line to the curve at the point $(-1,-3).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}+3{x}^{2} & =4xy \\\\ {y}^{2}+3{x}^{2}-4xy & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)={y}^{2}+3{x}^{2}-4xy$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=0.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(-1,-3)$ can be found by computing $∇f(-1,-3).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(-1,-3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{2}+3{x}^{2}-4xy)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}+3{x}^{2}-4xy)\\,j \\\\  & =(6x-4y)\\,i+(2y-4x)\\,j \\\\ ∇f(-1,-3) & =(6(-1)-4(-3))\\,i+(2(-3)-4(-1))\\,j \\\\  & =6\\,i-2\\,j \\\\  & =⟨6,-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇f(-1,-3)=⟨6,-2⟩$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(-1,-3).$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}} & =\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}} \\\\ \\frac{x-(-1)}{6} & =\\frac{y-(-3)}{-2} \\\\ (-2)(x+1) & =6(y+3) \\\\ -2x-2 & =6y+18 \\\\ 6y+2x+20 & =0 \\\\ 3y+x+10 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338936 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "923db4e3-1672-5a50-9581-8d103a4e9394" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x+2y-2=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337357} {:answer/id #uuid "a0ca54cd-1662-5f06-ad4a-139620f93cc6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-x+y-1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337484} {:answer/id #uuid "a201c603-2105-5c16-ac28-6ea1909ee0e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x+y-1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337500} {:answer/id #uuid "f0aa3752-46b2-55e2-872a-d4d7eb4de111" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x+y+1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338139} {:answer/id #uuid "f7b5b220-5b4e-5b43-8acc-b11f2fdd7f50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x-y+1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338205}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186337500} :answer-field/id #uuid "a093c235-9586-5c2c-a6e1-6f61aa48334d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338573}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "47afea57-2ac6-5bfd-8472-520b5199e74f" :question/math-academy-id "q-71393" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)=2xy.$  Find the equation of the normal line to the curve at the point $(0,1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y) & =2xy \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)-2xy & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)-2xy$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=0.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(0,1)$ can be found by computing $∇f(0,1).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(0,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)-2xy)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)-2xy)\\,j \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{x+y}-2y)\\,i+(\\frac{1}{x+y}-2x)\\,j \\\\ ∇f(0,1) & =(\\frac{1}{0+1}-2(1))\\,i+(\\frac{1}{0+1}-2(0))\\,j \\\\  & =-i+j \\\\  & =⟨-1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇f(0,1)=⟨-1,1⟩$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(0,1).$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}} & =\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}} \\\\ \\frac{x-0}{-1} & =\\frac{y-1}{1} \\\\ -x & =y-1 \\\\ x+y-1 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338938 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17754529-babb-5cf0-8d95-1b9cc9537939" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}\\,i+\\frac{3}{5}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336252} {:answer/id #uuid "2027b715-6614-5f66-82b2-1f71af6cb775" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}\\,i+\\frac{4}{5}\\,j+\\frac{1}{5}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336332} {:answer/id #uuid "36a67130-4f88-5f7d-9e66-e328e09a4789" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "i+j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336538} {:answer/id #uuid "530705a9-532d-591f-bbf7-492c24eb6728" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}\\,i+\\frac{4}{5}\\,j+k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336798} {:answer/id #uuid "8a797477-b113-50ff-8e91-509061d9a654" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}\\,i+\\frac{4}{5}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337280}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186337280} :answer-field/id #uuid "380e5863-1f99-5c29-acf5-cfab37a637d6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338378}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "7aa18077-2806-56e2-99dd-f7365f248356" :question/math-academy-id "q-71403" :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y,z)=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}$, find a vector that's perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(3,4,0).$" :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(3,4,0)$ can be found by computing \n$∇f(3,4,0).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient and evaluate it at $(x,y,z)=(3,4,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}})\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}})\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}\\text{ }i+\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}\\text{ }j+\\frac{z}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(3,4,0) & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{(3)^{2}+(4)^{2}+(0)^{2}}}\\,i+\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{(3)^{2}+(4)^{2}+(0)^{2}}}\\,j+\\frac{0}{\\sqrt{(3)^{2}+(4)^{2}+(0)^{2}}}\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}\\,i+\\frac{4}{5}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\frac{3}{5}\\,i+\\frac{4}{5}\\,j$ is perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(3,4,0).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338939 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "217b6b69-1258-52ca-92cb-84d166055cab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "i-\\frac{1}{2}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336344} {:answer/id #uuid "8847c9cc-20d2-574c-a780-1b2f9a38d565" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "i-\\frac{1}{2}\\,j+2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337260} {:answer/id #uuid "9b429560-704b-5928-a704-fbc5ba05c495" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "i-\\frac{1}{2}\\,j+\\frac{1}{2}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337439} {:answer/id #uuid "edd7d436-4244-565c-b127-7c866996e6d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-i-\\frac{1}{2}\\,j+2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338108} {:answer/id #uuid "f0dcdca1-0652-5268-9a5c-8bdbc0a1e227" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "i-2\\,j+2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338140}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186337260} :answer-field/id #uuid "b348b668-7c03-56ec-b406-92e416034172" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338605}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "7acbac43-bc81-5177-9def-8ee007ede43b" :question/math-academy-id "q-71404" :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({z}^{2}-xy)-yz$, find a vector that's perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,-2,0).$" :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,-2,0)$ can be found by computing \n$∇f(1,-2,0).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient and evaluate it at $(x,y,z)=(1,-2,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({z}^{2}-xy)-yz)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({z}^{2}-xy)-yz)\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({z}^{2}-xy)-yz)\\,k \\\\  & =-\\frac{y}{{z}^{2}-xy}\\text{ }i+(-\\frac{x}{{z}^{2}-xy}-z)\\text{ }j+(\\frac{2z}{{z}^{2}-xy}-y)\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(1,-2,0) & =-\\frac{(-2)}{(0)^{2}-(1)(-2)}\\,i+(-\\frac{1}{(0)^{2}-(1)(-2)}-0)\\,j+(\\frac{2(0)}{(0)^{2}-(1)(-2)}-(-2))\\,k \\\\  & =i-\\frac{1}{2}\\,j+2\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $i-\\frac{1}{2}\\,j+2\\,k$ is perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,-2,0).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338940 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "013cdae6-0c8a-5577-8cd4-30685db895d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{x-1}{9}=\\frac{y}{-9}=\\frac{z-3}{-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336043} {:answer/id #uuid "2c4309e0-a7c1-56bf-b10e-a0aad8914c08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{x-1}{9}=\\frac{y}{9}=\\frac{z+3}{-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336451} {:answer/id #uuid "4d059734-4e63-596b-82e0-ae11c512dc22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{x-1}{9}=\\frac{y}{-9}=\\frac{z+3}{-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336749} {:answer/id #uuid "bc72b63f-4d93-5d70-823f-f701135f625c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{x-1}{9}=\\frac{y}{-9}=z-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337706} {:answer/id #uuid "fc9ffc45-7093-5fd9-87c8-ae8b17f9efee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{x-1}{9}=\\frac{y}{9}=z+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338254}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186336749} :answer-field/id #uuid "7690b5ea-01a5-5497-9080-1cc6400cb01b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338481}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "5b6f2dff-79f7-5470-a733-d919ff7c2a24" :question/math-academy-id "q-71411" :question/problem "Find the equation of the normal line to the surface $3{x}^{2}-xz=6-3xyz$ at the point $(1,0,-3).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-xz & =6-3xyz \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-xz+3xyz & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y,z)=3{x}^{2}-xz+3xyz$, our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=6.$ \n\nA normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point $(1,0,-3)$ can be found by computing $∇f(1,0,-3).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(1,0,-3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3{x}^{2}-xz+3xyz)i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3{x}^{2}-xz+3xyz)j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(3{x}^{2}-xz+3xyz)k \\\\  & =(6x-z+3yz)\\text{ }i+3xz\\text{ }j+(-x+3xy)\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(1,0,-3) & =(6(1)-(-3)+3(0)(-3))\\text{ }i+3(1)(-3)\\text{ }j+(-1+3(1)(0))\\text{ }k \\\\  & =9\\,i-9\\,j-k \\\\  & =⟨9,-9,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y},{v}_{z}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}}=\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}}=\\frac{z-{z}_{0}}{{v}_{z}} \\\\ \\frac{x-1}{9}=\\frac{y-0}{-9}=\\frac{z-(-3)}{-1} \\\\ \\frac{x-1}{9}=\\frac{y}{-9}=\\frac{z+3}{-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338941 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c165a4b-7842-5080-b22d-dd998f4efc33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y\\operatorname{ln}⁡(yz+zx)\\text{ }i+y\\operatorname{ln}⁡(yz+zx)\\text{ }j+\\frac{xy}{z}\\text{ }k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336145} {:answer/id #uuid "546a4641-9ee2-54b1-9a19-949510d27139" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(z)\\text{ }i+y\\operatorname{ln}⁡(z)\\text{ }j+xy\\operatorname{ln}⁡(z)\\text{ }k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336813} {:answer/id #uuid "c3ad8a94-3343-5d3e-a1c5-41b03ebbd768" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y\\operatorname{ln}⁡(z)\\text{ }i+xy\\text{ }j+\\frac{xy}{z}\\text{ }k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337767} {:answer/id #uuid "d4f50290-6f7b-5795-9900-ef50d3f55007" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "xy\\operatorname{ln}⁡(z)\\text{ }i+xy\\operatorname{ln}⁡(z)\\text{ }j+\\frac{xy}{z}\\text{ }k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337892} {:answer/id #uuid "efe0a9c2-3996-5cde-90b0-22cd1b0fb18a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y\\operatorname{ln}⁡(z)\\text{ }i+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(z)\\text{ }j+\\frac{xy}{z}\\text{ }k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338130}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338130} :answer-field/id #uuid "f4a6e392-66e6-5d55-9d5a-95166bc83697" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338705}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c4f224f8-09b2-598d-bc90-7a090ce4820d" :question/math-academy-id "q-71422" :question/problem "Given $h(x,y,z)=xy\\operatorname{ln}⁡(z)$, find $∇h(x,y,z).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}_{x}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy\\operatorname{ln}⁡(z)) \\\\  & =y\\operatorname{ln}⁡(z) \\\\ {h}_{y}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy\\operatorname{ln}⁡(z)) \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(z) \\\\ {h}_{z}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(xy\\operatorname{ln}⁡(z)) \\\\  & =xy\\cdot \\frac{1}{z} \\\\  & =\\frac{xy}{z}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the gradient is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇h(x,y,z) & ={h}_{x}(x,y,z)\\text{ }i+{h}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{h}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =y\\operatorname{ln}⁡(z)\\text{ }i+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(z)\\text{ }j+\\frac{xy}{z}\\text{ }k\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338942 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a21b9f7-4aaa-5471-bf82-f2d8ab4a05d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(2x{y}^{2}{z}^{2}+{e}^{xyz})\\text{ }i+(2{x}^{2}y{z}^{2}+{e}^{xyz})\\text{ }j+(2{x}^{2}{y}^{2}z+{e}^{xyz})\\text{ }k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336422} {:answer/id #uuid "71cc0d72-27c7-5788-8341-714d4d503875" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(2x+yz{e}^{xyz})\\text{ }i+(2y+xz{e}^{xyz})\\text{ }j+(2z+xy{e}^{xyz})\\text{ }k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337049} {:answer/id #uuid "78722099-cc9a-5084-9630-ca6c5d2d30e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(2x{y}^{2}{z}^{2}+yz{e}^{xyz})\\text{ }i+(2{x}^{2}y{z}^{2}+xz{e}^{xyz})\\text{ }j+(2{x}^{2}{y}^{2}z+xy{e}^{xyz})\\text{ }k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337116} {:answer/id #uuid "7a839402-601f-5953-874b-788c1ff02eff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(x{y}^{2}{z}^{2}+yz{e}^{xyz-1})\\text{ }i+({x}^{2}y{z}^{2}+xz{e}^{xyz-1})\\text{ }j+({x}^{2}{y}^{2}z+xy{e}^{xyz-1})\\text{ }k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337136} {:answer/id #uuid "ac9d6b72-745e-56b9-86ea-4dab4db1e685" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(2x{y}^{2}{z}^{2}+x{e}^{xyz})\\text{ }i+(2{x}^{2}y{z}^{2}+y{e}^{xyz})\\text{ }j+(2{x}^{2}{y}^{2}z+z{e}^{xyz})\\text{ }k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337584}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186337116} :answer-field/id #uuid "df0b11fb-76fe-57a4-9209-9720c0dc1087" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338670}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "2d8f2390-7ede-5da9-be05-731bb79d1f06" :question/math-academy-id "q-71425" :question/problem "Find $∇g(x,y,z)$ given that  $g(x,y,z)={x}^{2}{y}^{2}{z}^{2}+{e}^{xyz}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}_{x}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}{y}^{2}{z}^{2}+{e}^{xyz}) \\\\  & =2x{y}^{2}{z}^{2}+yz{e}^{xyz} \\\\ {g}_{y}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}{y}^{2}{z}^{2}+{e}^{xyz}) \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}y{z}^{2}+xz{e}^{xyz} \\\\ {g}_{z}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}{y}^{2}{z}^{2}+{e}^{xyz}) \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}{y}^{2}z+xy{e}^{xyz}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,  the gradient is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇g(x,y,z) & ={g}_{x}(x,y,z)\\text{ }i+{g}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{g}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =(2x{y}^{2}{z}^{2}+yz{e}^{xyz})\\text{ }i+(2{x}^{2}y{z}^{2}+xz{e}^{xyz})\\text{ }j+(2{x}^{2}{y}^{2}z+xy{e}^{xyz})\\text{ }k\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338952 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23400f54-8b3f-5ebc-b598-c7818f4586c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y\\text{ }i+x\\text{ }j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336361} {:answer/id #uuid "34196f13-9af3-5e84-930f-7cdc02f6b4ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x\\text{ }i+y\\text{ }j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336521} {:answer/id #uuid "78b044f0-5569-5fa2-b926-c4fa16e63d1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\text{ }i+\\text{ }j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337118} {:answer/id #uuid "86424757-d1c0-5142-865b-bc737655d636" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\text{ }i+\\text{ }j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337246} {:answer/id #uuid "8a45146a-092c-5746-a1f7-52f22a8062eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\text{ }i-\\text{ }j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337277}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186337118} :answer-field/id #uuid "0fa16c79-d297-52a5-b07c-cccb5e15aa03" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338305}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "91443473-cbb1-5ab2-b98b-379cda895777" :question/math-academy-id "q-71476" :question/problem "Find the gradient of the product $f(x,y)h(x,y),$ given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ={x}^{2}y+x{y}^{2},\\, &  & ∇f(x,y)=(2xy+{y}^{2})\\text{ }i+({x}^{2}+2xy)\\text{ }j, &  &  \\\\ h(x,y) & =\\frac{1}{xy},\\, &  & ∇h(x,y)=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}y}\\text{ }i-\\frac{1}{x{y}^{2}}\\text{ }j. &  & \\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the product of two functions $f(x,y)$ and $h(x,y)$ is given by:\n\n$$\n∇[fh]=h\\,∇\\,f+f\\,∇\\,h\n$$\n\nUsing the product rule, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇[f(x,y)h(x,y)] & =h(x,y)∇f(x,y)+f(x,y)∇h(x,y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{xy}((2xy+{y}^{2})\\text{ }i+({x}^{2}+2xy)\\text{ }j)+({x}^{2}y+x{y}^{2})(-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}y}\\text{ }i-\\frac{1}{x{y}^{2}}\\text{ }j) \\\\  & =(\\frac{2xy+{y}^{2}}{xy}\\text{ }i+\\frac{{x}^{2}+2xy}{xy}\\text{ }j)+(-\\frac{{x}^{2}y+x{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}y}\\text{ }i-\\frac{{x}^{2}y+x{y}^{2}}{x{y}^{2}}\\text{ }j) \\\\  & =(\\frac{2x+y}{x}\\text{ }i+\\frac{x+2y}{y}\\text{ }j)+(-\\frac{x+y}{x}\\text{ }i-\\frac{x+y}{y}\\text{ }j) \\\\  & =(-\\frac{x+y}{x}+\\frac{2x+y}{x})\\text{ }i+(-\\frac{x+y}{y}+\\frac{x+2y}{y})\\text{ }j \\\\  & =\\text{ }i+\\text{ }j\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338958 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "095f0794-39e0-5248-a897-200aa365fb49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336115} {:answer/id #uuid "3b3334b6-e096-5561-85b8-d0e63cefc06f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336589} {:answer/id #uuid "67a09e4b-a7d7-5075-9742-255d4d1d82c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336957} {:answer/id #uuid "b9bb0e9e-f8a4-5eca-8ee8-abae6195f1c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337678} {:answer/id #uuid "c5c71b4a-acd4-54cc-98ec-eb1e46b34922" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337787}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186336589} :answer-field/id #uuid "90dbd918-3070-505f-8d67-8a3bd6620730" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338544}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "34e4c933-c949-5c6b-b0d7-3414a5ba0bf7" :question/math-academy-id "q-71527" :question/problem "Suppose $|\\,p\\,|=\\sqrt{3}$ and the angle between vectors $p$ and $q$ is $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$ Find the scalar projection of $p$ onto $q.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula for the scalar projection, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{q}\\,p & =|\\,p\\,|\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338959 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21ef2e36-7c06-57fe-a2e8-27848b286f5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336349} {:answer/id #uuid "32bc2709-4755-5539-a2ad-575115b181b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336512} {:answer/id #uuid "46863dc5-85a6-5f74-b07a-395c884b21c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336686} {:answer/id #uuid "cc073af2-ce94-51b6-9620-4721296451cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337822} {:answer/id #uuid "e0bd5231-b4ba-55f8-840d-4883556a842d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337994}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186337994} :answer-field/id #uuid "dbc5ca9d-1270-51e9-8a5b-a7cb066ec78d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338664}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "c0a3ab5c-4820-575a-9c45-dc5563c40e29" :question/math-academy-id "q-71530" :question/problem "Suppose $|\\,p\\,|=3\\sqrt{2}$ and the angle between vectors $p$ and $q$ is $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ Find the scalar projection of $p$ onto $q.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula for the scalar projection, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{q}\\,p & =|\\,p\\,|\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339099 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a942dfb1-22bb-50b2-98f2-430b4650b212" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨8,5⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,\\,y-2⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337559} {:answer/id #uuid "cfff2df5-b768-59eb-8a42-e88ece513035" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨5,10⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,\\,y-2⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337851} {:answer/id #uuid "d9eb05eb-aff7-5ff4-a1a3-c9872358aa4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨-10,15⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,\\,y-2⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337929} {:answer/id #uuid "f6a6cdf3-2c19-59a7-a21a-bd11e399318c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨15,10⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,\\,y-2⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338196} {:answer/id #uuid "fc4820c5-0c22-53d7-866f-01894b830fd0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨10,5⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,\\,y-2⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338247}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338247} :answer-field/id #uuid "acd1dce1-2f9e-5620-9964-a61a6eed12b9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338593}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "36039f0f-02eb-5e5b-b0c3-c0ef4b9dcb59" :question/math-academy-id "q-72371" :question/problem "Given the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}{y}^{2}+xy$, find the equation of the tangent line to the level curve that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(1,2).$" :question/worked-solution "The equation of the tangent line to the level curve $f(x,y)=k$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be expressed as \n\n$$\n∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0}⟩=0.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the gradient of $f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(x,y)=(1,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}{y}^{2}+xy)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}{y}^{2}+xy)\\,j \\\\  & =(2x{y}^{2}+y)\\,i+(2y{x}^{2}+x)\\,j \\\\ ∇\\,f(1,2) & =(2(1)(2)^{2}+(2))\\,i+(2(2)(1)^{2}+(1))\\,j \\\\  & =10\\,i+5\\,j \\\\  & =⟨10,5⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the Cartesian equation of the tangent line to the level curve that passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0}⟩ & =0 \\\\ ∇f(1,2)\\cdot ⟨x-1,\\,y-2⟩ & =0 \\\\ ⟨10,5⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,\\,y-2⟩ & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339107 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5a7f7daf-eab5-5f09-9994-fb78510d1452" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6\\,i+6\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336857} {:answer/id #uuid "5bdf6205-616a-5fe8-bae1-1eafc7069834" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "i-j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336867} {:answer/id #uuid "5f640f70-dd55-53af-a444-45f3d1013ded" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\,i-j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336897} {:answer/id #uuid "6d1b9f33-c38c-59c8-99f2-2467570782ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "i+6\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337000} {:answer/id #uuid "9f2d1ea8-f373-5f19-800d-34e70ba82c85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\,i-6\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337470}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186336857} :answer-field/id #uuid "31c46a4e-d03a-5a5a-b5e5-3a014deba440" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338371}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "148b9435-dbdc-5e2a-8580-1b7906c6cec6" :question/math-academy-id "q-72400" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=2xy+9.$ Find a tangent vector to that curve at the point $(-1,2).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =2xy+9 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2xy-9 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2xy-9$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=0.$ \n\nA tangent vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(-1,2)$ can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\nt(-1,2)={f}_{y}(-1,2)\\,i-{f}_{x}(-1,2)\\,j\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $t(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(-1,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2xy-9)\\,i-\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2xy-9)\\,j \\\\  & =(2y-2x)\\,i-(2x-2y)\\,j \\\\ t(-1,2) & =(2(2)-2(-1))\\,i-(2(-1)-2(2))\\,j \\\\  & =6\\,i+6\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our tangent vector is $6\\,i+6\\,j.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339110 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c870de58-6cf8-5f55-bffc-f699bc46f20d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id 